### 1. Preliminaries  

> “Define \(T_n=\{(a,b)\in\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\mid a+b\le n+1\}\). … A simple count gives \(|T_n|=\frac{n(n+1)}2\).”

- **Verification:** The set \(T_n\) indeed consists of all lattice points in the right‑angled triangle with vertices \((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\).  
  The count \(\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\frac{n(n+1)}2\) is correct.  
- **Conclusion:** Correct.

> “For a column \(x=c\) (\(1\le c\le n\)) the points of \(T_n\) … contain \(h_c=n+1-c\) points. In particular \(h_n=1,\;h_{n-1}=2,\;h_{n-2}=3\).”

- **Verification:** Direct from the definition; each column loses one point as we move right. The three values are correct.  
- **Conclusion:** Correct.

---

### 2. Points on a sunny line  

> “Let \(L\) be a sunny line that contains at least two points of \(T_n\). Write its reduced slope as \(m=\frac pq\) …”

- **Verification:** The reduction to coprime integers \(p,q\) with \(q>0\) and the exclusions \(p\neq0,\;p\neq -q\) correctly encode “not horizontal, not vertical, not slope \(-1\)”.  

> “If \((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)\in L\cap\mathbb Z^2\) then … \(x_2-x_1\) is a multiple of \(q\); write \(x_2-x_1 = q\,t\). Consequently \(y_2-y_1 = p\,t\).”

- **Verification:** Standard property of lattice points on a line of rational slope; correct.

> “Now consider the sum \(s=x+y\). … \(s_2-s_1 = (p+q)t\). Thus the values of \(s=x+y\) taken by integer points of \(L\) form an arithmetic progression whose common difference is \(|p+q|\).”

- **Verification:** Algebra is correct; the progression of \(x+y\) follows directly.

> “Since every point of \(T_n\) satisfies \(2\le s\le n+1\), the number of points of \(T_n\) that can lie on \(L\) is at most \(\bigl\lfloor\frac{(n+1)-2}{|p+q|}\bigr\rfloor+1 = \bigl\lfloor\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\bigr\rfloor+1 \le \bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{|p+q|}\bigr\rfloor+1.\)”

- **Verification:** The bound follows from the length of the interval \([2,n+1]\) divided by the step size \(|p+q|\). The inequality \(\lfloor\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\rfloor+1\le\lfloor\frac{n}{|p+q|}\rfloor+1\) is trivial.  

> “Because \(|p+q|\ge1\) … a sunny line can contain at most \(n+1\) points of \(T_n\); more importantly, **it can meet each column in at most one point** (otherwise two points of the same column would have the same \(x\)-coordinate, forcing the line to be vertical, which is non‑sunny).”

