o attain the bound (2.5) and to be disjoint from the others.\n\n---\n\n### 7.  Identification of the lines that attain the bound\n\n> **\u201cEquation (2.1) with \\(|p+q|=2\\) gives three possibilities:  \n> * \\(p=q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(1\\));  \n> * \\(p=-2,\\;q=1\\)\u2003(slope \\(-2\\));  \n> * \\(p=-1,\\;q=2\\)\u2003(slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  \n> For \\(k=2m+1\\) the corresponding lines are exactly the three lines listed in (10).  A direct substitution shows that each of them contains \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) and that the three point\u2011sets are disjoint.  No other sunny line can contain \\(m+1\\) points, because any other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\), and then (2.4) gives a strictly smaller upper bound.\u201d**\n\n*Analysis of the claim.*  \n- The three possibilities for \\(|p+q|=2\\) are correctly enumerated.  \n- For a given odd \\(k=2m+1\\) there is **exactly one** line of each of those three slopes that attains the bound; its intercept is forced by the condition that the line contain \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  The solution\u2019s wording \u201cthe three lines listed in (10)\u201d is misleading because (10) displays the concrete lines for the special case \\(k=3\\) (i.e. \\(m=1\\)).  The general lines are:\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -2x + (k+2),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac{k+2}{2},\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n  which indeed contain \\(m+1\\) points of \\(T_k\\) and are pairwise disjoint.  The solution does not provide this explicit description, nor does it justify why no other line (for example a line of slope \\(1\\) with a different intercept) can also contain \\(m+1\\) points.  The argument that \u201cany other choice would have \\(|p+q|\\ge3\\) or \\(|p+q|=1\\) with \\(q\\ge2\\)\u201d correctly shows that any *different* slope cannot reach the bound, but the uniqueness of the intercept for each admissible slope is left implicit.  Hence this part contains a **Justification Gap** (the conclusion is true, but the reasoning is incomplete).\n\n---\n\n### 8.  Consequence for the size of \\(k\\)\n\n> **\u201cThus, to cover \\(T_{2m+1}\\) with \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines we must use **only** the three lines (10).  Hence we need \\(2m+1\\le3\\); i.e. \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=3\\).\u201d**\n\n*Analysis.*  \nGiven the previous paragraph\u2019s (incomplete) justification that only three distinct sunny lines can attain the bound, the conclusion that we cannot have more than three sunny lines is logical.  Since each sunny line must be one of those three, the number of sunny lines \\(k\\) cannot exceed three.  Together with the earlier parity restriction (odd \\(k\\) only), this yields \\(k=1\\) or \\(k=3\\).  The deduction is sound **provided** the claim about the uniqueness of the three bound\u2011attaining lines holds, which we have argued is true but was not fully proved in the solution.  Therefore this step inherits the **Justification Gap** from the previous paragraph.\n\n---\n\n### 9.  Constructions for the admissible values\n\n> **\u201c* \\(k=0\\).  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non\u2011sunny and cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  \n> * \\(k=1\\).  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\).  The sunny line \\(\\ell:\\; y=2x-2n+1\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any forbidden direction.  \n> * \\(k=3\\).  Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The uncovered set is \\(T_3\\).  The three sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> \\begin{aligned}\n> L_{1}&: y=x,\\\\\n> L_{2}&: y=-2x+5,\\\\\n> L_{3}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52\n> \\end{aligned}\n> \\]\n> contain respectively the points \u2026 which are exactly the six points of \\(T_3\\).\u201d**\n\n*Verification of the constructions.*\n\n- **\\(k=0\\).**  Trivial and correct.\n\n- **\\(k=1\\).**  After removing the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) the only point left is \\((n,1